#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088563} :activity/id #uuid "c53676ea-f8fc-5605-ba89-a2a860fc10c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049233 :topic/id #uuid "e572abc9-c83b-5936-8447-f37bda127dca" :topic/math-academy-id 871} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088907 :tutorial/content "A **hyperbola** is a curve that takes the general form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {a^2} - \\dfrac {y^2} {b^2} = 1 \\quad \\textrm{or} \\quad \\dfrac {y^2} {a^2} - \\dfrac {x^2} {b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. (They represent the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes of the hyperbola, but you don't have to worry about that right now.)\n\nFor now, let's just look at an example of a graph of a hyperbola. Below, we have the graph of the hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{3^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9} - \\dfrac{y^2}{4} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a=3$ and $b=2.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc897baaebee742651a113fa6d6909ee7e7616ac1fbe0695e016b5334a94bd22.png)\n\nLet's list some properties of the graph:\n\n- There are two pieces in the graph. These two pieces are called **branches** of the hyperbola.\n\n- The branches are symmetric over the $x$-axis and over the $y$-axis.\n\n- There are two asymptotes (dashed lines in the diagram above).\n\n- There are exactly two $x$-intercepts, but there is no $y$-intercept. The graph does not intersect the $y$-axis at all.\n\n- The midpoint between the intercepts is the origin, $(0,0).$ In general, the midpoint between the intercepts is called the **center** of the hyperbola. In this case, we say that the hyperbola is centered at the origin.\n\nIn particular, the curve above is a **horizontal** hyperbola since the axis that intersects the graph ($x$-axis) is *horizontal*. \n\nThe **general standard equation** of a horizontal hyperbola is also known as the **Cartesian equation** and is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {a^2} - \\dfrac {y^2} {b^2} = 1.\n$$\n\n**Note:** When writing a hyperbola in standard form, it's not necessary to write the denominators as squares. For the hyperbola above, either of the following would be acceptable for standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{3^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2} = 1 \\quad \\textrm{or} \\quad \\dfrac{x^2}{9} - \\dfrac{y^2}{4} = 1.\n$$\n\nFor the sake of simplicity, in this lesson we'll write the denominators without squares, as shown by the equation on the right." :tutorial/id #uuid "90371f6e-9dd1-5014-b828-b3afde2ab76a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3372 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3a500f3a-5971-5138-8c06-359420219eee" :step/math-academy-id 18848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088908 :tutorial/content "Similar to horizontal hyperbolas, there are also **vertical hyperbolas.** For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{9} = 1\n$$\n\nis a vertical hyperbola since the axis that intersects the graph ($y$-axis) is *vertical*.\n\n![](images/45/4553b22f870489a5ed297ad241e951652502b5b027a520f24b475305b31aa292.png)\n\nThe general standard equation for a vertical hyperbola is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y^2} {a^2} - \\dfrac {x^2} {b^2} = 1.\n$$\n\n**Note:** To remember which general standard equation corresponds to which type of hyperbola (horizontal or vertical), we can use the following mnemonics.\n\n- A *horizontal* hyperbola intersects the $x$-axis, so the $x$ term comes first:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {a^2} - \\dfrac {y^2} {b^2} = 1\n$$\n\n- A *vertical* hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis, so the $y$ term comes first:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y^2} {a^2} - \\dfrac {x^2} {b^2} = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a0be3d0-8bef-5f75-b897-7d1e8d9b52f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3373 :tutorial/title "Vertical Hyperbolas"} :step/id #uuid "4b18a5fa-139e-53eb-b402-4844785834cd" :step/math-academy-id 18849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087524 :question/id #uuid "dad615d8-1f62-51a0-aa21-b93313e1bfbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5117"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfbe689c-ae04-5405-9a38-d80f10662a0b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348248}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Horizontal Hyperbola in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "572f95df-8a6c-5ecc-ab62-4a24b11a2e43" :step/math-academy-id 18851 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087611 :question/id #uuid "93259130-d7bd-5db7-9bd5-c83c80ceb874" :question/math-academy-id "e-6774"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f783fe8-8f52-5f93-adef-bf4bd030fea0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348072}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Vertical Hyperbola in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "f606c1a7-4d6f-504f-95d7-e4558475bcd0" :step/math-academy-id 25030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088867 :tutorial/content "The *reciprocal function*\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis also known as a *rectangular hyperbola* centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/14/14e18eed930ad0f4a6b83f3de1425d0ef33b67f55f2e86c5d48bf38b66ac8e4f.png)\n\nCompared to the hyperbolas from this lesson, the branches of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ are rotated relative to the axes.\n\nTo see the connection more clearly, let's first rewrite the equation without fractions:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x} \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad xy=1\n$$\n\nNow, define new coordinates by\n\n$$\nx=u-v \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad y=u+v.\n$$\n\nThese correspond to a $45^\\circ$ rotation *up to scaling*.\n\nThen, we get\n\n$$\nxy=1 \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad (u-v)(u+v)=1 \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad u^2-v^2=1,\n$$\n\nwhich matches the definition of a *horizontal hyperbola*.\n\n![](images/17/1769f71454bdb84d3f26e12c80a3181ac8a8497e668e2679bad054c31065142c.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fcd5a0ac-0322-5559-a6a8-817d41de3d6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15093 :tutorial/title "Rectangular Hyperbolas and the Reciprocal Function"} :step/id #uuid "db3335d3-974e-54c0-95c1-4ed0e28e8860" :step/math-academy-id 615998}] :activity/title "Equations of Hyperbolas Centered at the Origin" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087936}]}
